  "body": "Currently, if one wants to add a new referrer policy, one needs to update:\n- Fetch\n- Referrer Policy\n- Several places in HTML:\n  - \"The following terms are defined in Referrer Policy\"\n  - \"Referrer policy attributes\"\n  - The attributes index\n\nI think it would be ideal if HTML does not list any specific referrer policy values at all, and instead just delegates to either Fetch or Referrer Policy. When @sideshowbarker added the `<link as>` attribute in #1449, I made him follow a similar philosophy of just delegating to Fetch.\n\nThis is a bit different from Destination since we aren't doing case-sensitive matching. (Maybe we could change it? Seems kind of annoying churn though for implementations which have already started their work.) But we should be able to do similar things. The only potentially tricky part is figuring out how to define an enumerated attribute without a table of keywords mapping to states. But I'm sure we can wordsmith it.\n",
